sentAtToPAS': '2024-03-28T04:19:08.932Z', 'id': 28504, 'personId': 23954, 'title': 'PhD student', 'companyName': 'Max Planck Institute of Molecular Cell Biology and Genetics (MPI-CBG)', 'companyId': None, 'dateRange': 'start': 'year': 2006, 'month': 10, 'end': 'year': 2010, 'month': 10, 'locationName': None, 'description': '- High throughput screen analysis (RNAi screens, drug screens);
- Algorithm design and implementation;
- Computer programming (mainly C++ and Matlab) for image analysis and statistical data analysis;
- GUI applications and databases.', 'isDefault': False, 'url': None, 'organizationDescription': None, 'website': None, 'domain': None Vice President of Computational Science NeoGenomics Laboratories I am passionate about cancer biology and epigenetics, assay development and diagnostic tools, and tackling complex problems via Bioinformatics and engineering solutions. I leverage problem analysis and solving skills, data analysis, and algorithm/model development. I love working with talented and creative people in a multidisciplinary environment.   Intro: I am currently the Vice President of Computational Science at Neogenomics. I have over 20 years’ experience in Bioinformatics and data analysis, particularly in the field of assay development and sequencing analysis. I have over 5 years’ experience in industry, in the field of diagnostic tests, liquid biopsies and tumor stratification assays. I have experience in leading a global team of nearly 30 people, with members scattered in Europe and various US sites. I have co-developed several methods and assays, which resulted in publications, patents or approved products: I co-authored over 30 peer reviewed, high-profile research papers and I am an inventor listed in 4 patent applications (see resume). I have experience in managing complex, multi-disciplinary software products/projects/releases, in assay development from early design feasibility stage to market launch, including validation activities for CAP/CLIA, iCAP and MolDX submissions. I also have experience in developing software under design control. I have experience in the preparation and execution of MolDX submissions (RaDaR™ assay), and maintaining an assay (InVisionFirst®-Lung) after market launch.  Career: I consider myself a Bioinformatics expert, capable of being a global leader within a large organization as well as a leader within a more dynamic team. I can leverage my ability in bringing products into production or leading the development of new products, where I can apply my expertise in developing prototypes and earlier stage products towards validation, regulatory submission and market launch. I like working with an enthusiastic team of scientists and technicians, as well as interact with other areas of the business and senior leadership, to ensure cross-functional work and alignment towards common, ambitious goals, and achieve great things together to make a difference for patients and cancer care. As I like to often say to my people, I can focus, execute and deliver, but where I really thrive is in the space in between, where I can help filling gaps and building bridges. Cambridge, England, United Kingdom

